E={6: 'Wake. No… Hearing the slow steps. Feel the ear hairs, telling him about a toe lazily pulling like a roll ankle across that carpet behind, and again, again, again; closer but then close. The catch sound of a stumbling heel against the doorframe behind; the screen door opens with a tiny rodent squeal. The mellotron busies away in jazzy-rock frantic jigs; the tune escapes with the doors.', 8: '“Thomas! Come in you bastard!” Slurring speech and a falling mouth, fumbling words. Thomas’s wife, Ana, is wearing a scarlet red silk dress and a charcoal rabbit mask. She puts out her lipstick-smeared face, open below the mask and over a clicking zip light, pulling and puffing with Thomas.', 19: "“Something isn’t right,” Thomas mutters, his brows hanging like an ageing stern porch. His long nose beak casts a shadow point onto one of the dolls. He continues, “You wouldn’t have believed these dolls to… Wait, don’t tell me, Ana, did you…?” He lifts the doll with the red yarn hair, Noidy. Ana begins smiling as her rabbit mask looks up at him, her face now lying on the table. “Ana!?” He sees it: the red wax seal stamp under the doll's back, her shirt pulled away like a letter, but of a necromantic kind. He says again, “Ana!?”", 23: 'The heavy, wide step in carpet in the hallway, a scuff and plant, slowly. The air is sucked out of the room. “Who is that!?” Thomas interrogates the dark with a jumping defense.', 35: 'The record at some point stopped. He cannot feel anything; controlled by movement, he continues out onto the porch, opening the screen door. The rain roars with clouds ahead. The water rushes with the wind away from the porch. Thomas takes his seat in the folding chair, staring into the blue sky, grey-cloud-covered, turning to black.', 37: 'There is a red house that wasn’t there, a small little red house; it looks to be some kind of clean abandonment. It is alone, and apart from the surroundings, it feels wrong to him. He is called, a misty-eyed walk through the splintering liquid chill. He walks off into the hills. Night wants to fall upon the setting sun, wanting to tackle.', 39: '“Just that? Not even a goodbye? We cannot save ourselves, yet you don’t mistake yourself for damning another?” the whispering dark one asks him.', 45: 'Thomas is ghostly, with operation strings that yank him out of home. Stupidly following every hint of epinoia. Searchless, but giving himself to findings. Flashes of Ana strike without lightning above, her red smeared face below the black rabbit mask, smiling and saying, “Stay here, for a while.” Tears roll down past her contracting, contradictory weep smile. Each blue light strike over sunset night brings the weeping pitch of a broken instrument, out of tune, this haunting portrayal pane of her.', 53: 'He takes a Zippo lighter to flick on, journeying into a home without carpets, only splintering wood with construction marks below. He walks through this hall and into an end, where a picture hangs of little Noidy and her yarn red hair; it shows her in a corner, washed in a bright white camera-flash. He checks the corners; nothing. He looks for a door; nothing.', 0: 'Chill air spikes roll the skin from winding winds, whirling humidly. Rain like today forever, in the moment. Crescendo drops over the grass-and-concrete duel. Lost in the back of the head and the stone porch hang. Tobacco chimney smoke skinny and then wide, like a channel or stream from embers. Contemplative rainfall. Gentle falls from above. Surrounded; rolls before mountains, greenery frock swabs bury all. The dark, shadowy chin point; he leans in.', 14: "Ana sits on the green pillow cushion with yellowish-gold fur tails; another just like it next to her lies on the ground, and she pats knowingly with crossed legs and further puffs; puffs at the embers' glow dangling. She exhales as Thomas squats down with a table’s stability aid.", 17: '“So, you’re seeing this?” Ana says while leaning in, her face smushed by her hand she rests upon from the table, her cigarette still lit in the other hand; exhales barely rise above the writing below the dolls. The old record still sings mellotron melodies. Thomas nods.'}
